{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>broken-nosed or with one eye.</b> — \"חָרֻם\" is anyone whose nose-bridge is sunk between the two eyes, so that he paints both eyes as one; this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 21:18). In Torat Kohanim (parashah 3, sec. 7) and in the Gemara of Bekhorot (43a) we learned: חרום — whose nose-bridge is sunk; whose nose-bridge is flat (בלום), whose nose-bridge is running (סולד), whose nose-bridge drips — from where? Scripture says: \"or חרום.\" Abba Yose says: חרום means only one who paints both eyes as one. They said to him: you have gone to the extreme — even if he cannot paint both eyes as one. And the word חרום is from the language of \"כָּל חֵרֶם אֲשֶׁר יָחֳרַם\" (Leviticus 27:29), \"וְהַחֲרַמְתִּי אֶת עָרֵיהֶם\" (Numbers 21:2), the meaning being destruction, and he is called חרום because the nose is the splendor of the face, as they said (Yevamot 120a): they testify only on the face together with the nose, and one whose nose lacks the form of human beings — his form is destroyed (חרבה). And the sense of \"or גִבֵּן\" (Leviticus 21:20) is drawn to the word \"in his eye,\" meaning either he is a גבן in his eye, or thin or with a blemish in his eye — that is, his eyebrows (גבות) lie over his eyes, from the language of \"גַּב הַמִּזְבֵּחַ\" (Ezekiel 43:13), \"עַל גַּבִּי חָרְשׁוּ חֹרְשִׁים\" (Psalms 129:3). And Scripture mentioned first the blemishes involving missing limbs — blind or lame — then those involving smallness, such as the חרום, and those involving largeness, such as the שרוע, then bone fractures — even though all his limbs are present, once the bone is broken he is disqualified — and then it disqualified even for deformity of appearance, such as the גבן and one who has גרעינין in his eyes, and after that in the flesh of the body, for he must be clean and smooth, and then it added בעל רוח — one whose testicles are swollen with wind (בעל רוח) — even though this is an ailment common among the elderly and is not a blemish in bone or in flesh. And our Sages explained (Bekhorot 6:7) that many blemishes are derived from these, for those mentioned in the Torah are archetypes (אבות) for them:"], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {0 "Ramban quotes the Mishnah in Bekhorot 6:7 (which in our editions is Mishnah 7 of chapter 6) regarding the גבן blemish; he reads the word as connected to גב (ridge/brow) rather than to a curvature of the spine, following the reading also found in Sifra Emor 3:7."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:57:42.695437960Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}